# Scope: this file covers the two things a commit here can leak — credentials and
# real customer data — plus the generated output of the toolchain this project
# actually uses (Django + manage.py test, Create React App, npm, Postgres with a
# SQLite fallback, VS Code on Windows).
#
# Rules for tooling the project does not have were removed rather than kept "just
# in case": tox, nose, hypothesis, pytest, coverage, mypy, ruff, Yarn PnP, lerna,
# Eclipse, Visual Studio solutions and Python packaging never run here, so their
# rules could not match anything, and a rule that cannot match is noise that makes
# the ones that can harder to audit. Add them back alongside the tool, not before.

# ── Credentials & secrets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A bare name with no slash matches at every depth, so one line covers
# backend/credentials.json as well as the repo root.
credentials/
credentials.json
# GOOGLE_CREDS_PATH defaults to credentials.json (book_event/management/commands/
# sync_bookings_from_sheets.py); token.* is what an OAuth flow writes back beside
# it, and a refresh token is as sensitive as the client secret.
token.json
token.pickle
service-account*.json
*.pem
*.key
*.p12

# Every .md file, repo-wide, and deliberately so — this repo tracks no markdown
# at all, which is what keeps a CREDENTIALS.md or a pasted-in dump of connection
# strings from being committed by reflex. A doc that genuinely belongs in git
# needs `git add -f`. This is also why there is no separate CREDENTIALS.md rule:
# it was a subset of this one.
*.md

# ── Environment variables ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# .env* rather than a list of suffixes: settings.py reads config via
# python-decouple/dotenv, and a list covering .env and .env*.local would miss
# .env.production and .env.staging — the two most costly to leak. backend/.env
# is the live one; .env.example is the tracked template and is re-included.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ── Spreadsheets, documents & database dumps ────────────────────────────────
# Repo-wide, not backend/*.csv: that only ever matched the backend root, so an
# export saved one level down was staged as normal. Every one of these is real
# delegate, booking, paper and proposal data — the importers read .csv, .xlsx and
# .xlsm (accounts/import_common.py), historical_event_registry reads .pdf
# (import_2023_pdf and its siblings), and the export endpoints emit .csv. This is
# the largest body of personal data that can reach a commit. Nothing tracked
# matches; the importers read from outside the repo and the tests build their
# fixtures in memory. A sample file that truly belongs in git needs `git add -f`.
*.csv
*.xlsx
*.xls
*.xlsm
*.pdf
# pg_dump output from the local Postgres snapshot (see backend/pg.ps1), which is
# the whole CRM in one file. Archives are covered for the same reason — an
# exported dataset usually arrives zipped.
*.sql
# EXCEPT the hand-run schema files, which are the one kind of .sql that MUST be
# tracked. This project does not apply DDL with `manage.py migrate`: a schema
# change is delivered as a reviewed file under backend/sql/ containing the DDL,
# a rollback section and a verify section, applied by hand, and that same file
# inserts the row into django_migrations that marks the matching state-only
# Django migration as applied. So backend/sql/ IS the schema history — losing it
# leaves migration files that were never meant to run and no record of what was
# actually executed against the database.
#
# They were silently ignored until 2026-08-20; `git status` never mentioned
# them, because an ignored file is not reported as untracked. Caught by running
# `git check-ignore -v` over the directory rather than by anything noticing.
#
# Scoped to this one directory, and anchored with a leading slash, so the rule
# above still catches a pg_dump saved anywhere else — including one saved INTO
# backend/sql/ under any name that is not *.sql, and including any nested
# directory beneath it.
!/backend/sql/*.sql
*.dump
*.backup
*.pgdump
*.zip
*.7z
*.rar
*.tar
*.tar.gz

# ── Python ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# ── Django ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Machine-local settings overrides, which is where a local DB password ends up.
local_settings.py
# Postgres is the configured database, but settings.py keeps a SQLite fallback
# under DEBUG that writes backend/db.sqlite3 (settings.py:148). The second rule
# is its -journal / -shm / -wal sidecars, which the first does not match.
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite3-*
# Collected static and uploaded media are both generated. NOTE: the rule is
# `staticfiles/`, never `static/` — frontend/public/static holds real committed
# logo assets, and react-scripts' own output lands in frontend/build/static,
# which `build/` below already covers.
staticfiles/
media/
# Covers npm-debug.log and friends too, which is why there is no separate npm
# logs block.
*.log

# ── Node / React ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
node_modules/
build/
.eslintcache

# ── IDE & editor ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
*.code-workspace
*.swp
*.swo
# VS Code's Local History extension, which mirrors every save of every edited
# file — including one taken before a secret was moved out into .env.
.history/

# ── Scratch, backup and merge leftovers ─────────────────────────────────────
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*.tmp
*~

# ── OS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
.DS_Store
# A file literally named `nul`, which Windows creates when a command redirects
# to NUL from a POSIX shell (`… > /dev/null` is the portable form here).
nul

# One-file production fix scripts, run-time output
backend/fix_backup_*.json
backend/fix_report_*.md
backend/fix_source_*.xlsx

# docs/ is tracked; the repo ignores *.md globally on line 32
!docs/
!docs/*.md

# Generated by `manage.py preview_paper_review_email`, a local render for
# eyeballing the paper review email. Regenerate it, never commit it.
backend/paper_review_preview.html
